[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Encapsulation, Abstraction, Inheritance, and Polymorphism in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm based on the concept of "objects", which contain data and code: data in the form of fields (often known as attributes or properties), and code, in the form of procedures (often known as methods). C# is an object-oriented language, and it supports the four core principles of OOP: Encapsulation, Abstraction, Inheritance, and Polymorphism. Understanding these concepts is crucial for writing maintainable, reusable, and scalable C# code."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Encapsulation"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Encapsulation is the bundling of data (fields) and methods that operate on that data within a single unit, or "class". It also involves restricting direct access to some of the object's components, which is a means of preventing accidental modification of data. This is achieved through access modifiers like 'private', 'protected', and 'public'."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public class BankAccount {
  private string accountNumber;
  private decimal balance;

  public BankAccount(string accountNumber, decimal initialBalance) {
    this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
    this.balance = initialBalance;
  }

  public decimal GetBalance() {
    return balance;
  }

  public void Deposit(decimal amount) {
    if (amount > 0) {
      balance += amount;
    }
  }

  public void Withdraw(decimal amount) {
    if (amount > 0 && balance >= amount) {
      balance -= amount;
    }
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'accountNumber' and 'balance' are private fields, meaning they can only be accessed from within the 'BankAccount' class. The 'GetBalance', 'Deposit', and 'Withdraw' methods provide controlled access to the account's balance. This protects the data from being directly modified from outside the class, ensuring data integrity."},{"order":7,"type":"heading","text":"2. Abstraction"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Abstraction is the process of hiding complex implementation details and exposing only the essential information to the user. It allows you to focus on what an object does rather than how it does it. In C#, abstraction can be achieved using abstract classes and interfaces."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"public interface IShape {
  double GetArea();
  double GetPerimeter();
}

public class Circle : IShape {
  private double radius;

  public Circle(double radius) {
    this.radius = radius;
  }

  public double GetArea() {
    return Math.PI * radius * radius;
  }

  public double GetPerimeter() {
    return 2 * Math.PI * radius;
  }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'IShape' interface defines the contract for any shape, specifying that it must have 'GetArea' and 'GetPerimeter' methods. The 'Circle' class implements this interface, providing concrete implementations for these methods. The user only interacts with the 'IShape' interface, without needing to know the specific details of how the area and perimeter are calculated for a circle. This simplifies the use of the 'Circle' class and allows for easy substitution of other shapes that implement the same interface."},{"order":11,"type":"heading","text":"3. Inheritance"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inheritance is a mechanism that allows a new class (derived class or subclass) to inherit properties and behaviors from an existing class (base class or superclass). This promotes code reuse and establishes an "is-a" relationship between classes."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public class Animal {
  public string Name { get; set; }

  public virtual string MakeSound() {
    return "Generic animal sound";
  }
}

public class Dog : Animal {
  public override string MakeSound() {
    return "Woof!";
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'Dog' class inherits from the 'Animal' class. It inherits the 'Name' property and overrides the 'MakeSound' method to provide a specific implementation for dogs. This demonstrates how inheritance allows you to create specialized classes based on more general classes, reusing common functionality and adding or modifying specific behaviors."},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"4. Polymorphism"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Polymorphism means "many forms". In OOP, it refers to the ability of an object to take on many forms. This is typically achieved through inheritance and interfaces. C# supports two types of polymorphism: compile-time polymorphism (method overloading) and runtime polymorphism (method overriding)."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Method Overloading (Compile-time Polymorphism)"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Method overloading allows you to define multiple methods with the same name but different parameters within the same class."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"public class Calculator {
  public int Add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
  }

  public double Add(double a, double b) {
    return a + b;
  }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'Calculator' class has two 'Add' methods, one that takes two integers and another that takes two doubles. The compiler determines which method to call based on the types of the arguments passed."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Method Overriding (Runtime Polymorphism)"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Method overriding allows a derived class to provide a specific implementation for a method that is already defined in its base class. This is achieved using the 'virtual' and 'override' keywords, as shown in the Inheritance example."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"public class Animal {
  public virtual string MakeSound() {
    return "Generic animal sound";
  }
}

public class Dog : Animal {
  public override string MakeSound() {
    return "Woof!";
  }

  public class Cat : Animal {
    public override string MakeSound() {
      return "Meow!";
    }
  }

  public static void Main(string[] args) {
    Animal animal1 = new Animal();
    Animal animal2 = new Dog();
    Animal animal3 = new Cat();

    Console.WriteLine(animal1.MakeSound()); // Output: Generic animal sound
    Console.WriteLine(animal2.MakeSound()); // Output: Woof!
    Console.WriteLine(animal3.MakeSound()); // Output: Meow!
  }
}"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, even though 'animal2' and 'animal3' are declared as 'Animal' objects, the 'MakeSound' method that is called is the one defined in the 'Dog' and 'Cat' classes, respectively. This is because the 'MakeSound' method is declared as 'virtual' in the 'Animal' class and overridden in the derived classes. This allows you to treat objects of different classes in a uniform way, while still allowing them to exhibit their specific behaviors."},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"By understanding and applying these four principles of OOP, you can write more robust, maintainable, and reusable C# code."}]
